From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lu-Fi <lutz@fiebach.de>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 00:30:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] MIPS: xburst: gpio: re-arm both-edge emulation until stable;
 gpio-keys: recheck a pressed key

The XBurst1 GPIO controller (T10/T15/T20/T21/T23/T30/T31) has no dual
edge trigger.  IRQ_TYPE_EDGE_BOTH, which gpio-keys requests, is emulated
in gpio_mask_and_ack_irq() with the level trigger opposite to the
current pin level: sample the level, write PAT0, then clear the flag.
When the pin moves between the sample and the flag clear (a fast second
press, contact bounce), the trigger is left armed for the level the pin
already has and the flag of that edge has just been cleared.  The next
edge is then never seen: on a T23 a short reset-key press stayed "down"
in evdev until the next press (one press held 4.8 s, one > 20 s, which
let thingino-button arm the 20 s factory-reset action).

- gpio: sample, arm, clear the flag and sample again; re-arm (bounded)
  until both samples agree, so every edge after the last sample raises
  a new flag.
- gpio-keys: while a GPIO key reads as pressed, re-read its level every
  50 ms from the existing timer/work.  The input core drops repeated
  identical states, so this only emits an event when a release was
  lost.  Idle cost is zero; the timer is torn down on disable/remove.

Signed-off-by: Lu-Fi <lutz@fiebach.de>
---
diff --git a/arch/mips/xburst/soc-t10/common/gpio.c b/arch/mips/xburst/soc-t10/common/gpio.c
--- a/arch/mips/xburst/soc-t10/common/gpio.c
+++ b/arch/mips/xburst/soc-t10/common/gpio.c
@@ -447,18 +447,33 @@
 	struct jzgpio_chip *jz = irqdata2jz(data);
 	int pin  = data->irq - jz->irq_base;
 
-	if (irqd_get_trigger_type(data) != IRQ_TYPE_EDGE_BOTH)
-		goto end;
+	int level, tries = 0;
 
-	/* emulate double edge trigger interrupt */
-	if (gpio_pin_level(jz, pin))
-		writel(BIT(pin), jz->reg + PXPAT0C);
-	else
-		writel(BIT(pin), jz->reg + PXPAT0S);
-
-end:
 	writel(BIT(pin), jz->reg + PXMSKS);
-	writel(BIT(pin), jz->reg + PXFLGC);
+
+	if (irqd_get_trigger_type(data) != IRQ_TYPE_EDGE_BOTH) {
+		writel(BIT(pin), jz->reg + PXFLGC);
+		return;
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * Emulate a double edge trigger with the level trigger opposite to
+	 * the current pin level.  The level is sampled before the trigger is
+	 * re-armed and the flag cleared; if the pin moves in that window
+	 * (a fast second press, contact bounce), the trigger ends up armed
+	 * for the level the pin already has and the flag of that edge has
+	 * just been cleared, so the next edge (the key release) is lost.
+	 * Sample again after the flag clear and re-arm until both samples
+	 * agree; any edge after the last sample then raises a new flag.
+	 */
+	do {
+		level = gpio_pin_level(jz, pin);
+		if (level)
+			writel(BIT(pin), jz->reg + PXPAT0C);
+		else
+			writel(BIT(pin), jz->reg + PXPAT0S);
+		writel(BIT(pin), jz->reg + PXFLGC);
+	} while (gpio_pin_level(jz, pin) != level && ++tries < 8);
 }
 
 static int gpio_set_type(struct irq_data *data, unsigned int flow_type)
